            These seven are ranked on how their protein type behaves in coffee, protein per serving, ingredients and certification status, not on our own kitchen tests or a tasting panel; our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page explains the method. Two are collagens for hot coffee, two are unflavored isolates, and three are flavored or hydrolyzed wheys for lattes and iced drinks. Figures come from each label at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="can-you-put-protein-in-coffee" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Can You Put Protein Powder in Coffee?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Protein powder in coffee is safe, it does not make the protein useless, and it is one of the easiest ways to add 15 to 30 g of protein to a breakfast that would otherwise be mostly caffeine. The drink has picked up the nickname proffee on social media, but the idea is old: people have been stirring milk powder and gelatin into hot drinks for a century. What goes wrong is almost always texture, not safety or nutrition.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem is that most protein powders were designed to be shaken into cold water or milk. Pour a scoop of ordinary whey into a mug of coffee that has just come off the brewer and the outside of each clump of powder sets on contact, sealing dry powder inside. You get floating lumps, a skin on top and gritty bits at the bottom. That is a mixing problem, and it has three solutions: use a protein type that tolerates heat, cool the coffee slightly, or pre-mix the powder in something cool before it meets the heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whether it is worth doing depends on what you want. To hit a daily protein target, a scoop of whey isolate in coffee is as good as the same scoop in a shaker. For a creamier cup with a few extra grams, collagen is easiest. For a shake that tastes like coffee, buy a coffee-flavored powder and drink it cold. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">guide to how much protein you need</a> will tell you whether the extra grams matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone sensitive to caffeine should watch coffee-flavored powders that add their own caffeine. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or take medication that interacts with caffeine, check your protein and caffeine intake with a doctor or registered dietitian.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-it-clumps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Protein Powder Clumps in Hot Coffee
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two things happen when whey meets very hot liquid. The first is simple wetting: dry powder hydrates on the outside faster than water can penetrate the middle, so each pinch forms a gel shell around a dry core. That happens in cold water too, which is why shaker bottles exist, but heat speeds it up dramatically. The second is denaturation. The main whey protein, beta-lactoglobulin, starts to unfold at around 70 to 75 degrees Celsius, about 158 to 167 degrees Fahrenheit, and unfolded proteins stick to each other and set into curds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fresh drip coffee is well above that line. The Specialty Coffee Association's brewing guidance calls for water around 90 to 96 degrees Celsius, roughly 195 to 205 degrees Fahrenheit, and a mug poured straight from the pot is often still above 80 degrees Celsius. That is why whey in fresh coffee curdles and whey in coffee that has sat for five minutes often does not. It is also why a latte works better than black coffee: steamed milk is usually served around 60 to 65 degrees Celsius, under the point where whey sets quickly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Coffee and Protein by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">90-96°C</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical coffee brewing water temperature</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~70°C</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Where whey proteins start to set</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~60°C</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">A safer target for stirring in whey</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~95 mg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Caffeine in a serving of Premier Cafe Latte (listing)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Collagen peptides behave differently because they have already been hydrolyzed into short chains, so they dissolve in hot coffee and stay dissolved as it cools, without setting into a gel. That is why collagen became the default coffee protein. Casein and plant proteins are the worst offenders: micellar casein thickens and clumps, and pea or rice protein stays gritty at any temperature.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="collagen-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Collagen vs Whey Isolate vs Coffee-Flavored Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            These three types are not interchangeable, and the right one depends on whether you care more about convenience or nutrition. Collagen peptides win on mixing and lose on protein quality. Whey isolate wins on protein quality and needs a little care in hot coffee. Coffee-flavored whey wins on taste and convenience, but it is designed for cold drinks and often brings sweeteners and caffeine you may not want in a mug of coffee you have already sweetened.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hot coffee</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein quality</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Taste impact</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best use</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Collagen peptides</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dissolves easily</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Incomplete; low leucine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None if unflavored</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Daily hot mug</td>
                  </tr>
